Learn You Phext
Start here.

This file is a phext: plain text with addresses. It teaches phext by being one. Every idea in it lives in a scroll, every scroll lives at a coordinate, and you can fetch any scroll on its own without reading the rest.

If you are reading this through SQ, you just used the whole trick: a table of contents (toc) told you this coordinate exists, and a select fetched exactly this scroll.

How to read this file:
- Get the table of contents and pick a coordinate.
- Select one scroll at a time. Each scroll stands on its own.
- Part 3 is the machinery. Part 4 is practice. Part 5 is the principles and your turn to write.

This demo is non-personal teaching material, written for anyone. It is MIT licensed (Part 5), and it is the built-in phext for the public read-only demo exocortex.Why this exists: the Exocortex principles

An exocortex is memory that lives outside a single head and outside a single machine: text, addressed so that people and agents can find it again, share it, and build on it.

This demo follows five principles, and so does everything we build on it:

1. Free: no toll on reading, learning, or building.
2. Open: the format and the tools are inspectable. This file is plain text all the way down.
3. Decentralized: no single server is the exocortex. Anyone can host a copy of this file, and the copies are equal.
4. Trusted: claims carry receipts. When this file states a fact about phext, you can test it against the file itself.
5. Interoperable: phext is an encoding layer, like ASCII and UTF-8 before it. Any tool that can read text can start reading phext.

Hold every phext tool, this one included, to those five words.License: MIT

