The same text ships beside this file as LICENSE, so the license survives even if the scrolls are rearranged.What is a phext?

A phext is plain text where chunks of text carry addresses.

Ordinary text gives you position inside one stream: this line, then the next. Phext keeps that and adds structure on top: the text is cut into scrolls, and each scroll sits at a coordinate, so a reader (human or agent) can jump straight to one chunk, quote it, or replace it, without dragging the whole file along.

Phext is an interoperable encoding layer, in the same family as ASCII and UTF-8: it says how characters are arranged and addressed, not what they must mean. Meaning is up to the minds writing and reading.

Invented by Will Bickford. This file is one phext: the lesson and the example at the same time.Scrolls: the unit of phext

A scroll is a run of plain text between delimiters. It is the smallest addressed unit in a phext: coordinates name scrolls, and tools fetch, replace, and count scrolls.

Three facts to keep:

1. A scroll is just text. Read it with any text tool and it reads fine.
2. A scroll that uses none of the phext structure, no delimiters at all, is still valid: it is one scroll of plain text at the first coordinate. Phext degrades to plain text, never below it.
3. Scroll content is text, so a phext is valid UTF-8 within each scroll. Arbitrary binary does not ride raw; it rides as text (for example, base64) inside a scroll.

You are reading a scroll right now. Its coordinate is 1.1.1/1.1.1/2.1.2.Coordinates: addresses inside one file

A coordinate looks like this: 1.1.1/1.1.1/2.1.2. Nine numbers in three groups of three. It names exactly one scroll.

The rule that matters most: coordinates are internal to a phext.

The coordinate scheme is shared; anyone can read the address format. But an address only identifies a scroll within its own file. The coordinate 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1 in this file is the welcome scroll. The same coordinate in a different phext is a different scroll in that file, and means nothing here.

So a full reference is always a pair: which phext, plus which coordinate. Leave out the file and the address dangles.

A page number only means something once you name the book. Coordinates work the same way, and that locality is a feature: files stay self-contained and portable.Delimiters: where one scroll ends

Scrolls are separated by delimiter bytes: single ASCII control characters inside the text. There are nine, arranged from the largest division to the smallest:

Library 0x01, Shelf 0x1F, Series 0x1E, Collection 0x1D, Volume 0x1C, Book 0x1A, Chapter 0x19, Section 0x18, Scroll 0x17.

You will never see them in a normal reading view; renderers hide control bytes. But they are really in the file: open this phext in a hex editor and you can find them between scrolls.

What a delimiter does: it ends the current scroll, moves one step along its dimension, and resets every smaller dimension back to 1. A chapter delimiter starts a new chapter at its first scroll. A scroll delimiter just starts the next scroll.

Delimiters are personal: where a mind cuts its stream is personal to that mind's history. The format fixes the bytes; it does not fix where you must cut.The dimensions, counted honestly

Start with plain text. A page of text already has two dimensions: position along a line, and line down the page. Every text file you have ever read lives in those two.

Phext adds nine structural dimensions on top, one per delimiter level: library, shelf, series, collection, volume, book, chapter, section, scroll.

Nine plus two is eleven: phext is an 11-dimensional plain hypertext format.

A coordinate only writes the nine structural dimensions (for example 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1), because the other two belong to the text inside the scroll, addressed the way text always is: by line and by position along the line.

Most writing uses only the smallest few dimensions, the way this file mostly uses chapter and scroll. The rest is headroom: sparse space a growing corpus can expand into without renumbering what already exists.Anatomy of a coordinate

Take 1.1.1/1.1.1/3.1.3, the coordinate of this scroll.

Read it as three groups of three:

- First group (library.shelf.series): the library-scale container. Almost always 1.1.1 in a small phext, like this one.
- Second group (collection.volume.book): the middle shelves. Part 5 of this file lives in book 2, at coordinates like 1.1.1/1.1.2/1.1.1, because a book delimiter (0x1A) moved us there and reset everything below it.
- Third group (chapter.section.scroll): the fine address you will use daily. This file is organised by chapter: one chapter per part, scrolls inside it.

Coordinates are 1-indexed: counting starts at 1, and the first scroll of any phext sits at 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1.

Sparse is normal. Nothing requires coordinates to be dense or consecutive; empty space between scrolls is room to grow, not an error.Reading a phext with SQ

SQ is the reference tool for hosting a phext. It loads the file, serves its table of contents, and fetches scrolls by coordinate.

With a phext hosted, a visitor can ask for:

- toc: every coordinate in the file, each with a preview of its scroll. The map of the whole phext in one call.
- select with a coordinate: the full text of that one scroll, nothing else.
- status: which phext is hosted, its size in bytes, and how many scrolls it holds.

Try it on this file: ask for the toc, find 1.1.1/1.1.1/4.1.2, and select it. That scroll is this lesson worked as an example.

Reading is the default posture of the public demo: anyone may toc and select. Nobody needs the whole file to learn from one scroll.A worked example: selecting one scroll

Goal: read only the delimiters lesson, nothing else.

1. Ask SQ for the toc. Among the lines you will find one for 1.1.1/1.1.1/3.1.1, previewed with its first words: Delimiters: where one scroll ends.
2. Select that coordinate. SQ returns the scroll text alone: the delimiter table, the reset rule, and the note that delimiters are personal.
3. Notice what did not happen: the other fifteen scrolls stayed where they were. You spent one small request instead of downloading and searching a whole file.

That is the everyday phext move: toc to navigate, select to read. Coordinates turn a text file into an address space: stable places you can point at, link to, and return to.Writing and updating a single scroll

Reading is half the story. A phext is written scroll by scroll:

- To create or replace a scroll, write its text at its coordinate. Through SQ that is the update operation: the new text overwrites exactly that scroll, and the file is saved. Every other scroll is untouched.
- To add text to an existing scroll without replacing it, SQ offers insert, which appends to that scroll.
- To write a brand-new phext, write plain text and place delimiter bytes where scrolls should end. Any tool that can emit the control bytes, or a small script, can author one. This file was built that way.

One honest cost: SQ saves by rewriting the whole phext file on each change. Fine for books and demos; something a very large, busy phext has to plan around.

The public demo is read-only, so try writing on your own copy. That is the point of MIT: take the file; it is yours to change.Empty space and growing a phext

Most of a phext's coordinate space is empty, and that is by design.

Because higher delimiters reset the smaller dimensions, and because coordinates need not be consecutive, you can leave whole chapters, books, or volumes unclaimed and fill them years later. Existing scrolls keep their coordinates; growth does not renumber the past.

Two habits follow:

- Address by meaning, not by packing. Put a scroll where it belongs in the structure, even if its neighbours are far away.
- One fact per scroll, roughly. Fine-grained scrolls are what make toc, select, and single-scroll updates fast and legible.

This demo uses 16 scrolls in a space that could hold vastly more. The emptiness is not waste. It is the room the format promises.Phext principles, in one place

- Phext is an interoperable encoding layer: characters at addresses, like UTF-8 and ASCII before it. It constrains arrangement, not meaning.
- A scroll using none of the structure is still plain text. Phext never makes text less readable than it was.
- Coordinates are internal to a phext. The scheme is shared; the addresses are local to each file.
- Delimiters are personal. Where a mind cuts its stream is personal to that mind's history.
- Phext is valid UTF-8 within each scroll. It is a text format, end to end.
- Neuralese is a format riding phext, not phext itself. Formats can layer on the encoding layer without becoming it.

And the house rules for everything built here: MIT licensed; free, open, decentralized, trusted, interoperable.Make your own phext

You now know enough to start:

1. Write some text. Split it into chunks you might want to address on their own.
2. Join the chunks with the scroll delimiter (0x17). Start a new chapter with the chapter delimiter (0x19) when the topic turns. That is a valid phext already.
3. Host it with SQ, ask for the toc, and select a scroll back. If the coordinates match where you meant to be, you have written phext correctly.
4. Grow it in the empty space. Add scrolls at new coordinates; never renumber the old ones.

Everything in this demo, including this file, is MIT licensed, so your starting point can be this very text: copy it, cut it where your own history says to cut, and write on.Colophon

Learn You Phext, version 1, 2026-11-01.

Built as the built-in phext for the public read-only demo exocortex. Written from scratch as teaching material: no scroll in this file is copied from any personal phext, log, or correspondence. That exclusion is deliberate and permanent for this demo: a teaching file should be safe to hand to a stranger.

Structure: 16 scrolls. Part 1 (welcome, principles, license) is chapter 1 of book 1; Parts 2 to 4 are chapters 2 to 4 of book 1; Part 5 (principles in one place, make your own, this colophon) is in book 2, reached by a book delimiter, so the file demonstrates the hierarchy it teaches.

